{"causes":["The aftermath of World War II fostered a climate of questioning traditional gender roles.","The existentialist movement, which emphasized individual experience and freedom, laid the groundwork for de Beauvoir's philosophical inquiries.","The lack of comprehensive feminist literature prior to the 20th century created a void that The Second Sex sought to fill."],"effects":["The Second Sex catalyzed the second wave of feminism in the 1960s and 1970s, leading to significant social and legal reforms.","It inspired numerous feminist theorists and activists who expanded upon de Beauvoir's ideas, leading to the establishment of gender studies as a legitimate academic field.","The book's critique of patriarchy has influenced various social justice movements beyond feminism, including LGBTQ+ rights and intersectional advocacy."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"simone-de-beauvoir","sourceName":"Simone de Beauvoir","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"the-second-sex","targetName":"The Second Sex","context":"Simone de Beauvoir authored The Second Sex, published in 1949, as a critical analysis of women's oppression."},{"sourceSlug":"feminism","sourceName":"Feminism","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"the-second-sex","targetName":"The Second Sex","context":"Feminism as a movement played a crucial role in shaping the themes and discussions present in The Second Sex."},{"sourceSlug":"les-temps-modernes","sourceName":"Les Temps Modernes","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"the-second-sex","targetName":"The Second Sex","context":"Some chapters of The Second Sex were first published in the journal Les Temps Modernes during the late 1940s."},{"sourceSlug":"paris-france","sourceName":"Paris, France","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"the-second-sex","targetName":"The Second Sex","context":"The Second Sex was written and published in Paris, France, a center of existentialist thought."},{"sourceSlug":"women's-suffrage","sourceName":"Women's Suffrage Movement","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"the-second-sex","targetName":"The Second Sex","context":"The Women's Suffrage Movement's successes in the early 20th century set the stage for the conversations de Beauvoir engaged with in The Second Sex."}],"places":[{"name":"Paris, France","role":"Publication site"},{"name":"New York, USA","role":"Influential reception location"},{"name":"London, UK","role":"Major feminist discourse center"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: The Second Sex","type":"Reference article","slug":"the-second-sex"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q470098","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Second_Sex"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"The Second Sex contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Second Sex (French: Le Deuxième Sexe) is a foundational work of feminist philosophy and social theory by the French existentialist philosopher and novelist Simone de Beauvoir (1908–1986), published in two volumes in Paris on 5 June 1949 by Gallimard. The work consists of two parts: Volume I, 'Facts and Myths' (Faits et mythes), which analyses the biological, psychoanalytic, Marxist, and historical constructions of 'woman'; and Volume II, 'Lived Experience' (L'Expérience vécue), which analyses woman's concrete situation — her childhood, adolescence, sexual initiation, marriage, motherhood, and aging — from a phenomenological perspective. The book's opening declaration — 'One is not born, but rather becomes, a woman' ('On ne naît pas femme: on le devient') — is one of the most cited sentences in feminist philosophy and the foundational statement of the social constructionist approach to gender.

The Second Sex applies Sartrean existentialist philosophy (freedom, situation, bad faith, the Other) to the situation of women — arguing that woman has been constituted as the 'Other' (the Second Sex) relative to man (the Subject, the Absolute), denied the possibility of authentic self-determination by a patriarchal culture that defines femininity as passivity, immanence, and dependence rather than transcendence and freedom. De Beauvoir's analysis draws on existentialism (Sartre), Marxism (Engels), psychoanalysis (Freud, Adler), anthropology (Lévi-Strauss), and literary criticism (analyses of Montherlant, D. H. Lawrence, Claudel, Breton, and Stendhal's constructions of femininity in literature) to build a comprehensive account of women's oppression and its cultural mechanisms.

The Second Sex caused an immediate scandal in France — the Vatican placed it on the Index Librorum Prohibitorum, Sartre's companion Albert Camus accused de Beauvoir of making Frenchmen look ridiculous, and Gallimard printed two editions within the first week — and became one of the most widely read and influential texts of the 20th century, directly shaping the second-wave feminist movement of the 1960s–1970s.","subjects":["French Philosophy","Modern Era","Simone de Beauvoir","Feminism","Existentialism","Gender Theory","Social Theory","20th Century"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Second Sex significantly shaped feminist thought and activism, leading to the second wave of feminism and influencing gender studies as an academic discipline, with lasting implications on discussions of gender and equality.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
